5.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The corner is three black fragments A2 {A1,A3,B2}, B3B4 {A3,A4,B2}, C1C2 {B1,B2}. White has B5 and the B1/B2 liberty pair of C1C2.

1.W B1 is the first threat to the C1C2 liberty pair. 2.B B2 is legal and connects A2,B3,B4,C1C2 into M = A2B2B3B4C1C2 with L A1,A3,A4 and W B1 with L A1. 2.B A1 is also a defence, capturing the attacking B1 stone and leaving A1A2 {A3,B2}, B3B4 {A3,A4,B2}, C1C2 {B1,B2}. After the forced connection the merged mass keeps three liberties and the white B1 stone is in atari on A1. The classic A3–A4 net White tries to make is a net with outside liberty A5 because White already has B5. Black can therefore answer any A3/A4 atari with A5 and recapture the white net, restoring A3/A4 to Black and keeping at least one black group alive. The A5 snap-back is the decisive counter to the 1.W B1 lines, so B1 is not forcing.

1.W B2 puts C1C2 in atari on B1 and puts the new white stone in atari on B1. 2.B B1 is forced – otherwise 2.W B1 captures C1C2 outright – and it captures the white B2 stone and joins B1 to C1C2. After 2.B B1
X = B1C1C2 {A1,B2}
Y = A2 {A1,A3,B2}
Z = B3B4 {A3,A4,B2}
The three fragments share A1,A3,A4,B2. 3.W A3/A4 is forcing. The usual defence 4.B B2 merges X,Y,Z into M = A2B1C1C2B2B3B4 with L A1,A4 and White A3 with L A4. 5.W A4 connects the white A3–A4 net. Pre-securing A5 before the net is made prevents the A5 recapture that makes the 1.W B1 line fail. With A5 secured the merged mass is reduced to the single liberty A1 and White captures on A1. The 4.B A4 capture of White A3 is a tempo loss and is met by 5.W B2 / 5.W A5 re-establishing the net, and 4.B A1 is met by 5.W B2. Hence B2 forces a capture of the whole corner against every Black reply.

Non-adjacent first points let Black play B1/B2 immediately and keep C1C2 alive. The dispute in the current round is B1 vs B2 only vs no kill. The claim that 2.B B2 is illegal suicide after 1.W B1 is false – after the connection M has A1,A3,A4. The claim that 4.B B2 is illegal after 1.W B2 2.B B1 is also false – B2 is empty after the capture and connects the three fragments with liberties A1,A3,A4. The A5 recapture defence works after 1.W B1 but can be neutralised after 1.W B2 by first securing A5. Current evidence points to B2 as the unique forcing killing initiator; B1 is refuted by the A1 capture of the attacker and the A5 snap-back of any A3–A4 net. Unresolved is the exact ordering of A5 vs A3/A4, but the existence of a forcing first move is centred on B2.

1.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The board after the given stones is A2 b, B3 b, B4 b, C1 b, C2 b ; B5 w, C3 w, C4 w, D1 w, D2 w, D5 w. C1–C2 lives on B1,B2, A2 lives on A1,A3,B2, B3–B4 lives on A3,A4,B2. The only first points that touch the C1–C2 two-liberty chain are B1 and B2 up to symmetry.

1.W B1 forces 2.B B2, otherwise 2.W B2 captures C1–C2. After 2.B B2 M = A2 B2 B3 B4 C1 C2 has L_M = {A1,A3,A4} and W B1 has L = {A1}. The white stone is in atari on A1 and the A3–A4 net that white needs has the outside liberty A5 because A5 is linked to white B5. 3.W A3/A4 gives White the A3–A4 net with L = {A5}. Black can answer 4.B A1 capturing the B1 stone, or 4.B A4 capturing the white A3 stone, and in every case 6.B A5 captures the white A3–A4 net and restores A3,A4 to black. The early 3.W A5 line that was invoked to deny the snap-back still allows 4.B A1 to remove the B1 stone and leave M L = {A3,A4,B1}; White’s subsequent A3/A4 reduction is then met by 6.B A5 which recaptures the net and gives M back A3/A4. No line with 1.W B1 defeats the A5 recapture together with the A1 capture of the attacking B1 stone.

1.W B2 puts C1–C2 and the new white stone both in atari on B1. 2.B B1 is forced, it captures the white B2 and joins B1–C1–C2. After 2.B B1
X = B1C1C2 L {A1,B2}
Y = A2 L {A1,A3,B2}
Z = B3B4 L {A3,A4,B2}
3.W A3/A4 removes A3/A4 from Y/Z and puts the white A3/A4 stone in the A5 eye. The only move that keeps two liberties for the black material is 4.B B2, which merges X,Y,Z into M = A2 B1 C1 C2 B2 B3 B4 with L_M = {A1,A4} while the white A3 stone is L {A4}. 5.W A4 connects A3–A4, removes A4 from M and leaves M with the single liberty A1. 6.W A1 captures the merged mass. The 4.B A4 capture of the white A3 stone is a tempo loss, it is met by 5.W B2 which re-establishes the A3–A4 net, and 4.B A1 is met by 5.W B2 as well. The A5 snap-back that defeats the B1 attack is no longer available because the capture at B1 has already removed one of the interior liberties and the white net now has the outside liberty A5 that is permanently linked to B5.

Non-adjacent first points allow Black to occupy B1/B2 immediately and keep C1–C2 alive. The {} / none position is the extreme reading of the A5 defence for the B1 line, and the {B1,B2} claim fails once the A1 capture of the B1 stone and the subsequent A5 recapture are taken into account. The current evidence therefore favours B2 as the unique first move that initiates a plan that defeats every Black defence. Unresolved in the discussion is the exact sequencing of the A5 denial for B1, but the A5 recapture is consistently available after 1.W B1.

